The ClientHello laid out as quic-go lays it out (hysteria client).

quic-go cuts the ClientHello in one place and one place only: the second half of SNI
(vendor/quic-go/crypto_stream.go:193-195). It writes everything else in order, skipping over that
piece (:255-282), and hands the piece out once writeOffset has reached the end of the ClientHello
(:221-253) — so it goes out last, in the final Initial packet of the flight, and a DPI that
reassembles CRYPTO frames in arrival order sees a truncated name. The packet is filled with CRYPTO
frames while it has room (vendor/quic-go/packet_packer.go:648-657), and its frames are then
shuffled uniformly (rand.Shuffle, :1067-1071).

Upstream ngtcp2 crumbles instead: conn_crumble_initial_crypto cuts the ClientHello at random points
into up to eleven pieces, appends PING and PADDING frames between them, and permutates them with
ngtcp2_pkt_permutate_vec — which draws j from [0, i), a Sattolo shuffle, so no piece ever stays
where it was. The SNI it treats two ways: when everything fits in one packet it splits the vector at
the middle of the name, and when it does not, ngtcp2_pkt_remove_vec_partial removes a random piece
of 1..30 bytes starting at that middle and conn_cut_crypto_frame pushes it back into the send queue
— where its low offset makes it go out FIRST in the next packet, not last. A dozen frames where
quic-go writes three, cut in places quic-go does not cut, and the piece held back arrives early
rather than late.

This patch replaces the crumbling with quic-go's layout: conn_scramble_initial_crypto ends a CRYPTO
frame where the second half of SNI begins and resumes where it ends, takes that piece out of the
send queue and holds it in conn->initial_crypto_cut until the queue runs dry, then writes it, and
shuffles the frames of the packet uniformly. The pieces of upstream's scheme that nothing uses any
more (ngtcp2_pkt_split_vec_rand, ngtcp2_pkt_split_vec_at, ngtcp2_pkt_append_ping_and_padding,
ngtcp2_pkt_permutate_vec, ngtcp2_pkt_remove_vec_partial) go with it.

Two things quic-go does that this patch deliberately leaves out: the second cut it makes in the ECH
extension (crypto_stream.go:196-201), which this client would never reach because its ClientHello
carries no ECH extension, and PADDING written before the frames instead of after them
(packet_packer.go:1064-1066), which is the layout of the packet rather than of the CRYPTO stream and
belongs with the padding and packet number work.

